{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-140541",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "We're given the **rate of change** of the account's balance $r(t).$ To find the **net change** in the balance between the times $t=2$ and $t=4,$ we integrate $r(t)$ between these two limits. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{4}20{e}^{0.05t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={20\\,\\frac{{e}^{0.05t}}{0.05}|}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =400{e}^{0.05t}{|}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =400({e}^{0.2}-{e}^{0.1}) \\\\  & ≈46\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer. \n\nTherefore, between years $t=2$ and $t=4,$ the account's balance increased by $$46\\,000.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
